ct":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"01 natural sciences"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"&lt;span&gt;A machian model as potential alternative to dark matter halo thesis in galactic rotational velocity prediction&lt;/span&gt;","subTitle":null,"descriptions":null,"publicationDate":"2025-01-01","publisher":"Elsevier BV","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":null,"container":{"name":"SSRN Electronic Journal","issnPrinted":null,"issnOnline":"1556-5068","issnLinking":null,"ep":null,"iss":null,"sp":null,"vol":null,"edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_________::4eab3cb8d180c217315458b76d131f47","originalIds":["10.2139/ssrn.5080965","50|doiboost____|4eab3cb8d180c217315458b76d131f47"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.2139/ssrn.5080965"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":0.0,"influence":2.1746283E-9,"popularity":2.2497804E-9,"impulse":0.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Université Catholique de Louvain","acronym":"UCL","id":"openorgs____::73e124d9d4f37ce3a694e2b09faf0f28","pids":[{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"100007370"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100005043"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"100007355"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q378134"},{"scheme":"OrgReg","value":"BE0007"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100005041"},{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"398478"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q378134"},{"scheme":"PIC","value":"999980664"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100008135"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"100007355"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/02495e989"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100008135"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"865419266"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"000000012294713X"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100005041"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100005043"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"100007370"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.7942.8"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"95674353"}],"countries":[{"code":"BE","label":"Belgium"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.uclouvain.be/en-index.html"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.2139/ssrn.5080965"}],"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5080965"],"publicationDate":"2025-01-01","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"issn__online::40ce99e275066b606ac04ff2dc8de65a","value":"SSRN Electronic Journal"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"inDiamondJournal":false,"green":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Jin Lim","name":"Jin","surname":"Lim","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":"orcid_______::1ee0b7584bcbb1898afc9a6123d2251f","fullName":"Sungeun Kim","name":"Sungeun","surname":"Kim","rank":2,"pid":{"id":{"scheme":"orcid","value":"0000-0002-7558-8502"},"provenance":null}}],"openAccessColor":"hybrid","publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"eng","label":"English"},"countries":null,"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0103 physical sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"01 natural sciences"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"Scaling relations between mass components and rotational velocity in disk galaxies","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>                <jats:p>In this study, we re-evaluate the baryonic Tully–Fisher relation (BTFR) by analyzing the correlations between maximum rotational velocity and various mass components, including stellar mass, atomic hydrogen (H i) mass, baryonic mass, and dark matter mass in a sample of 141 disk galaxies from the SPARC (Spitzer Photometry and Accurate Rotation Curves) database, augmented by recent data on stellar and dark matter masses. We apply multiple statistical methods, including Monte Carlo orthogonal distance regression (MCODR), Monte Carlo least-squares (MCLS), and traditional least-squares (LS), to assess the impact of different fitting techniques on the derived scaling relations between the mass components (stellar, H i, and dark matter) and maximum rotational velocities of these galaxies. We find that the selection of statistical methods significantly influences the derived slopes and intercepts the relation between maximum rotational velocity and mass components. The MCODR method that accounts for errors in both variables consistently produces steeper slopes, suggesting a stronger correlation between stellar mass and rotational dynamics compared to other methods. In contrast, the MCLS method tends to yield flatter slopes, highlighting the sensitivity of this approach to outliers. Despite the variations in slope and intercept across different methods, the fundamental relation between baryonic mass and rotational velocity remains robust. We have also compared dark matter mass derived from different halo models [NFW (Navarro–Frenk–White) versus combined NFW + Dekel–Zhao profiles] and noted that the slope from the NFW profile is slightly steeper than that from the combined profile, highlighting the sensitivity of scaling relations to the selection of halo model. Overall, this study reinforces the robustness of the BTFR across different mass components in disk galaxies while emphasizing the critical role of statistical methods and dark matter profiles in analyzing galactic dynamics.</jats:p>"],"publicationDate":"2024-11-06","publisher":"Oxford University Press (OUP)","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["Crossref"],"formats":null,"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":{"name":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","issnPrinted":"0004-6264","issnOnline":"2053-051X","issnLinking":null,"ep":"1348","iss":null,"sp":"1336","vol":"76","edition":null,"conferencePlace":null,"conferenceDate":null},"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"doi_________::d34aa838401a3484e76fa6a3d829c56f","originalIds":["10.1093/pasj/psae092","50|doiboost____|d34aa838401a3484e76fa6a3d829c56f"],"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.1093/pasj/psae092"}],"dateOfCollection":null,"lastUpdateTimeStamp":null,"indicators":{"citationImpact":{"citationCount":0.0,"influence":2.1746283E-9,"popularity":1.944044E-9,"impulse":0.0,"citationClass":"C5","influenceClass":"C5","impulseClass":"C5","popularityClass":"C5"}},"projects":null,"organizations":[{"legalName":"Sejong University","acronym":"Sejong University","id":"openorgs____::70fcaeff31a50f8ba68b3e40853e4a00","pids":[{"scheme":"OrgRef","value":"28817546"},{"scheme":"ISNI","value":"0000000107276358"},{"scheme":"FundRef","value":"501100002546"},{"scheme":"fundref","value":"501100002546"},{"scheme":"GRID","value":"grid.263333.4"},{"scheme":"Wikidata","value":"Q491711"},{"scheme":"mag_id","value":"28777354"},{"scheme":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/00aft1q37"},{"scheme":"wikidata","value":"Q491711"}],"countries":[{"code":"KR","label":"Korea (Republic of)"}],"websiteurl":"http://www.sejong.ac.kr/"}],"communities":null,"collectedFrom":[{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}],"instances":[{"pids":[{"scheme":"doi","value":"10.1093/pasj/psae092"}],"license":"OUP Standard Publication Reuse","accessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/","openAccessRoute":"hybrid"},"type":"Article","urls":["https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/psae092"],"publicationDate":"2024-11-06","refereed":"peerReviewed","hostedBy":{"key":"issn___print::abc613f9a6560219808f1d0b8f854bd1","value":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan"},"collectedFrom":{"key":"openaire____::081b82f96300b6a6e3d282bad31cb6e2","value":"Crossref"}}],"links":[],"otherTitles":null,"inDiamondJournal":false,"green":false,"isGreen":false,"isInDiamondJournal":false},{"authors":[{"id":null,"fullName":"Aceña, Andrés Esteban","name":"Andrés Esteban","surname":"Aceña","rank":1,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Barranco, Juan","name":"Juan","surname":"Barranco","rank":2,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Bernal, Argelia","name":"Argelia","surname":"Bernal","rank":3,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"López, Ericson","name":"Ericson","surname":"López","rank":4,"pid":null},{"id":null,"fullName":"Llerena, Mario","name":"Mario","surname":"Llerena","rank":5,"pid":null}],"openAccessColor":null,"publiclyFunded":false,"eoscIfGuidelines":null,"type":"publication","language":{"code":"eng","label":"English"},"countries":[{"code":"AR","label":"Argentina","provenance":null}],"subjects":[{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"EQUATION OF STAT"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"STABILITY"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"DARK MATTER"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"0103 physical sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"https://purl.org/becyt/ford/1.3"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"https://purl.org/becyt/ford/1"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"FOS","value":"01 natural sciences"},"provenance":null},{"subject":{"scheme":"keyword","value":"GALACTIC HALO"},"provenance":null}],"mainTitle":"A further analysis for galactic dark matter halos with pressure","subTitle":null,"descriptions":["Spherically symmetric and static dark matter halos in hydrostatic equilibrium demand that dark matter should have an effective pressure that compensates the gravitational force of the mass of the halo. An effective equation of state can be obtained for each rotational velocity profile of the stars in galaxies. In this work, we study one of this dark matter equation of state obtained for the Universal Velocity Profile and analyze the properties of the self-gravitating structures that emerges from this equation of state. The resulting configurations explaining the observed rotational speeds are found to be unstable. We conclude that either the halo is not in hydrostatic equilibrium,or it is non spherically symmetric, or it is not static if the Universal Velocity profile should be valid to fit the rotational velocity curve of the galaxies.","Fil: Aceña, Andrés Esteban. Consejo Nacional de Investigaciones Científicas y Técnicas. Centro Científico Tecnológico Conicet - Mendoza. Instituto Interdisciplinario de Ciencias Básicas. - Universidad Nacional de Cuyo. Instituto Interdisciplinario de Ciencias Básicas; Argentina","Fil: López, Ericson. Escuela Politécnica Nacional; Ecuador","Fil: Bernal, Argelia. Universidad de Guanajuato; México","Fil: Barranco, Juan. Universidad de Guanajuato; México","Fil: Llerena, Mario. Universidad de La Serena; Chile"],"publicationDate":"2021-12-01","publisher":"Cornell University","embargoEndDate":null,"sources":["reponame:CONICET Digital (CONICET)","instname:Consejo Nacional de Investigaciones Científicas y Técnicas"],"formats":["application/pdf"],"contributors":null,"coverages":null,"bestAccessRight":{"code":"c_abf2","label":"OPEN","scheme":"http://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/documentation/access_rights/"},"container":null,"documentationUrls":null,"codeRepositoryUrl":null,"programmingLanguage":null,"contactPeople":null,"contactGroups":null,"tools":null,"size":null,"version":null,"geoLocations":null,"id":"dedup_wf_002::d3c0f46d47593f6b92c0e5d5838f4de0","originalIds":[